MVM.registerModule({
  "id": "m21",
  "summary": "Diagnose faults with a systematic method, use service information and tools well, serve customers honestly, keep clear records, cost jobs correctly and keep developing as a professional.",
  "outcomes": ["Apply the six-step diagnostic method and avoid replacing parts by guesswork.","Use repair information, scan tools, a multimeter and a scope, and record measurements.","Listen to customers, explain faults in plain words and give honest estimates.","Write a clear job card and handle parts, warranties and records correctly.","Calculate labour, estimates and invoices, and reduce comebacks.","Plan your professional development and work safely, ethically and legally."],
  "cheatsheet": ["The six steps: verify the complaint, gather information, check the obvious, test and isolate, repair, confirm.","A symptom is not a cause. Test before you replace a part.","Compare readings with the manual and with a known-good vehicle.","Write down measurements and results while you work, not afterwards.","Explain the fault in plain words, then give the price and get approval before extra work.","A job card records the complaint, the findings, the work, the parts and the test result.","Estimate = labour hours × rate + parts + tax, with a margin for the unknown.","Comebacks come from rushed diagnosis, poor parts, missed final checks and poor communication.","Learn new vehicle technology all the time. A good mechanic is always a student."],
  "lessons": [
    {"id": "m21-l1", "title": "Systematic diagnosis", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A good diagnostician finds the cause of a fault quickly and with few wasted parts. A poor one replaces parts until the fault goes away, and the customer pays for it. The difference is a method."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"diagnostic-flow"},
      {"t":"h","text":"The six steps"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["**Verify the complaint.** Ask the customer, then see or hear the fault yourself, if you can do so safely. A fault you cannot reproduce is hard to fix.","**Gather information.** Read the history, scan for codes, look for bulletins, and ask when it began, what changed and under what conditions.","**Check the obvious.** Fluids, fuses, connectors, battery condition, leaks, loose parts and the simple things that cause many faults.","**Test and isolate.** Use the manual to plan tests that divide the system into halves. Measure, and compare with the specification.","**Repair.** Fix the cause, not only the symptom, and use the correct parts and the correct torque.","**Confirm.** Repeat the test drive or the test and check that the fault is gone, and that no new fault appears."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Symptoms and causes"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A symptom is what the customer notices, for example 'the car is hard to start'. A cause is the fault in the part, for example a weak battery, a bad connection or a clogged filter. Many different causes can give the same symptom. So **do not replace a part until a test shows it is faulty**."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Avoiding parts-swapping"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Swapping parts is expensive, takes time and can hide the real fault. It also teaches nothing. Before you replace a part, you should be able to say: 'I measured this value, the specification is this, so this part is faulty.' A code from the scan tool is **a clue, not a verdict**: a sensor code can come from the sensor, the wire, the connector, the ECU or the real fault that the sensor sees."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Divide and conquer"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Most systems can be split into a chain: input, wiring, control unit, output. Test in the middle of the chain first. If the signal is good at the middle, the fault is further along. If it is bad, it is before the middle. Each test halves the search. Ten parts can be narrowed to one in about four tests, instead of ten."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Narrowing a no-start","steps":["The engine cranks but does not start. The chain is: fuel, air, spark, compression, and the signals that tell the computer when to act.","Test 1: is there a spark? Yes. That removes the ignition system.","Test 2: is there fuel pressure? No. That points to the fuel pump, relay, fuse, supply wiring or the filter.","Test 3: is there power at the pump connector when the key is turned on? Yes, 12 V. The supply is good, so the fault is the pump itself or the filter.","Test 4: measure the pressure after the filter and before it. The pressure is zero before the filter too, so the pump is faulty. Replace it and confirm."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Test before you replace. A confirmed fault gives a lasting repair and a happy customer."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Keep a notebook with the vehicle, the symptom, what you tested and what you found. It makes the next case faster and helps you explain your work."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m21-l2", "title": "Diagnostic case studies", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The best way to learn diagnosis is to follow real cases from complaint to cure. Each case below uses the six steps. Notice that the cause is often not where the symptom shows, and that a measurement, not a guess, decides each step."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Case 1: A slow crank and a clicking sound"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Symptom: the engine turns slowly, then only clicks","steps":["**Verify:** the customer is right. The starter turns slowly and the dash lamps dim.","**Obvious checks:** the battery terminals have white corrosion. The battery voltage at rest is 12.2 V (a charged battery reads about 12.6 V).","**Test:** with the headlamps on, crank and watch the voltage. It falls to 8.1 V (it should stay above about 9.6 V). Then measure the voltage drop across the positive cable and across the earth cable during cranking. The earth drop is 0.8 V; it should be under 0.2 V.","**Isolate:** the high drop on the earth side means a poor connection. The cable end is corroded under the insulation.","**Repair:** clean the terminal, replace the earth cable and tighten, then charge the battery and load test it.","**Confirm:** the crank voltage is now 10.4 V and the drop is 0.1 V. The engine starts at once."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Case 2: The engine runs rough at idle with the light on"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Symptom: rough idle, engine light on, code for a lean mixture on bank 1","steps":["**Gather:** the long-term fuel trim is +22% (a normal range is within about ±10%). The computer is adding fuel to make up for extra air.","**Obvious checks:** no leaks visible, the air filter is clean.","**Test:** the trim improves from +22% to +5% at 2,500 rpm. Extra air at idle but not at higher speeds suggests a small air leak. Use a smoke machine on the intake. Smoke comes from a cracked vacuum hose behind the manifold.","**Repair:** replace the hose. Clear the adaptations and codes.","**Confirm:** the trim is now within ±3% and the idle is smooth. A code for a lean mixture does not mean that a sensor is bad. It only reports what the sensor saw."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Case 3: An intermittent no-start"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Symptom: the car starts for days, and then will not start on some mornings","steps":["**Gather:** it does not depend on the weather. It sometimes starts after waiting 10 minutes. No codes are stored.","**Plan:** an intermittent fault needs data when it happens. Connect a scan tool to log the crankshaft signal, and leave a note for the customer to bring the car in when it fails again.","**Test when failed:** fuel pressure is good, spark is missing, and the scan tool shows no engine speed signal during cranking.","**Isolate:** the crankshaft sensor gives no signal. Wiggling its connector brings the signal back. The pins in the connector are loose.","**Repair:** clean and tighten the pins, or replace the connector. Then secure the harness.","**Confirm:** wiggle test and restarts after being heated and cooled. A common fault in an intermittent complaint is a connection, not a part."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Case 4: A vibration that appears at 90 km/h"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Symptom: the steering wheel shakes at 90 km/h and not at 70","steps":["**Verify:** road test confirms a shake at 80 to 100 km/h.","**Obvious checks:** tyre pressures, wheel nuts and visible tyre damage are all normal.","**Test:** a front-wheel vibration in the steering wheel is usually from the front wheels. Balance each front wheel on a balancer: the left needs 45 g, the right is within limits.","**Repair:** balance the left tyre correctly. Check for a bent rim or a bulge in the tyre.","**Confirm:** road test again. If a shake remains, check the wheel bearings, tie rods and the road-force reading."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"What the cases show"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Every case began with a check of the simple things: battery, connections, air leaks, wheel balance.","Every case used a measurement and a specification, not a feeling.","In each case the code or symptom pointed to an area, not to a part.","Every case ended with a confirming test."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Work from the symptom to the area, from the area to a test, and from the test to the part."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"When you finish a difficult job, write the case in your notebook in the same six steps. Within a year you will have your own reference book."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m21-l3", "title": "Using information and tools", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Modern vehicles are too complex to repair from memory. The skill is knowing where to find the right information and how to use the right tool."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Information sources"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Workshop manual and repair database:** the main source for specifications, procedures and torque figures.","**Wiring diagrams:** show how a circuit is connected and how to test it.","**Technical service bulletins (TSBs):** the maker's notes on known faults and fixes.","**Recall notices:** safety defects that the maker repairs free of charge. Always check them.","**Forums and colleagues:** useful for ideas, but check any advice against the manual."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Tools"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Tool","Use"],"rows":[["Scan tool","Reads codes, live data, freeze frame and performs tests and coding"],["Multimeter","Measures voltage, resistance and current, and for voltage drops"],["Oscilloscope","Shows a signal as a graph over time, such as a sensor, injector or CAN bus"],["Pressure and vacuum gauges","Check fuel, oil, boost, compression and vacuum"],["Smoke machine","Finds leaks in intake, EVAP and exhaust systems"],["Thermal camera or thermometer","Finds hot and cold spots, such as a dragging brake or a blocked cooler"]],"caption":"Diagnostic tools"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Good habits"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Use **data logging** to capture a fault that comes and goes, then study the record at your desk.","Compare with a **known-good** vehicle or part if you can.","Write the readings, the conditions and the date.","Know your limits. If a fault is in an area that you are not trained for, such as high voltage or airbags, **escalate** to a senior or a specialist."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Always check the battery voltage and the earths before blaming a computer. Low voltage and bad connections cause a great many strange faults on modern cars."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Take care of the tools themselves. A scan tool needs updated software, a meter needs a good battery and clean leads, and a scope needs correct probes and settings. A tool that reads wrongly leads to wrong decisions."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A tool shows data. You must understand what the data should be. Know the normal value before you read the actual value."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Photograph the screen of the scan tool and the setup of your test. It is an easy record for the job card."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m21-l4", "title": "Electrical and data diagnosis in practice", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Most faults on a modern vehicle involve electricity somewhere: a sensor, a connector, a wire or a module. A technician who can measure voltage, resistance and signals with confidence can solve problems that stop others. This lesson gives the basic electrical tests and the way to read live data."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The voltage drop test"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A wire or a switch that looks fine can still waste power if it has a poor connection. A **voltage drop test** measures the loss while the circuit is working. It is more useful than a resistance test, because the circuit is carrying its normal current."},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Voltage drop = current × resistance of the part","caption":"Measure with the circuit switched on and carrying current. Place the meter across the part, not across the load."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Circuit","Acceptable drop"],"rows":[["Positive cable during cranking","Under about 0.3 V"],["Earth cable during cranking","Under about 0.2 V"],["Lighting or other power circuits","Under about 0.3 V in total"],["Sensor signal or reference circuits","Under about 0.1 V"],["A single connector or switch","Under about 0.1 V"]],"caption":"Always compare with the figure in the workshop manual if one is given."},
      {"t":"example","title":"A dim headlamp","steps":["A headlamp draws 4.6 A. Battery voltage at the lamp is 12.6 V at the battery and 11.3 V at the lamp: a loss of 1.3 V.","Measure across each part of the circuit: the fuse 0.05 V, the switch 0.1 V, the earth wire 1.15 V. The earth wire holds most of the loss.","Resistance of the earth path = 1.15 ÷ 4.6 = 0.25 Ω, far too high. The earth connection is corroded. Clean and tighten it, and the lamp is bright again."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Common electrical faults and tests"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Fault","Symptom","Test"],"rows":[["Open circuit","A part does not work at all","Test for voltage on each side of the break, or measure the resistance with the power off"],["High resistance","A part works weakly, or slowly","Voltage drop test while it is working"],["Short to earth","A fuse blows. A wire is touching metal.","Measure the resistance from the wire to earth with the power off, and disconnect parts one at a time"],["Short to voltage","A part stays on, or a sensor reads wrongly","Check voltage on a wire which should be off"],["Parasitic drain","A flat battery after standing","Measure the current with the vehicle asleep. Pull fuses one at a time to find the circuit."]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Reading live data"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A scan tool shows what the computer sees. Compare it with what you know should be true."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Parameter","Typical warm idle","If wrong"],"rows":[["Coolant temperature","85 to 105 °C","A reading of −40 °C or 140 °C is an open or shorted sensor"],["Short-term fuel trim","Swinging around 0%","Stuck at one end shows a mixture fault"],["Long-term fuel trim","Within about ±10%","High positive means lean, high negative means rich"],["Engine load","About 20 to 35%","High load at idle suggests a leak or a restriction"],["Battery voltage","13.8 to 14.5 V","Low means a charging fault, high means a regulator fault"],["Throttle position","About 0 to 10% at idle","Not smooth when the pedal moves means a worn sensor"]]},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Look at the **freeze frame** stored with a fault code: speed, load, temperature. It tells you the conditions when the fault happened.","Compare values that should agree, for example two throttle sensors, or air temperature with coolant temperature on a cold engine.","Use **graphs** to see changes. A flat line or a spike is easy to see on a graph.","Use **bidirectional controls** to switch an actuator on and off from the tool, and check it responds."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Using an oscilloscope"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An oscilloscope draws voltage against time. It shows what a meter hides: fast changes, drop-outs, noise and the shape of a signal. Set the voltage scale so that the whole signal fits, and the time scale so that you see a few cycles."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Signal","A good one looks like","A bad one looks like"],"rows":[["Crankshaft sensor (Hall)","A square wave with clean edges and a gap for the missing tooth","Rounded edges, missing pulses or noise"],["Oxygen sensor","Switching up and down quickly around 0.45 V","Slow, flat or stuck at one level"],["Injector","A clean drop to earth, then a small spike when it closes","No spike, or a pulse that is too short or too long"],["CAN bus","Two mirror-image signals, between about 2.5 V and 3.5 V and 1.5 V and 2.5 V","A flat line or a distorted pattern show a network fault"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Measure under load, measure the voltage drop, and compare the live data with what should be true."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never probe an airbag circuit, a high-voltage cable or the orange cables of an electric vehicle. Use only the test methods that the manual allows. Back-probe a connector, and never pierce wire insulation."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m21-l5", "title": "Customer service", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Customers do not only pay for a repair. They pay for trust. A mechanic who listens, explains and is honest will keep customers for many years."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Listening"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Greet the customer politely and let them tell the story without interruption.","Ask useful questions: When did it start? Is it hot or cold? Does it happen at a certain speed? Have there been recent repairs?","Repeat the complaint in your own words to confirm you understood it.","Take notes, and write the customer's words on the job card."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Explaining"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Use plain words and avoid jargon. Compare to everyday things: 'The brake pads are like the soles of shoes and are worn through.' Show the old part if you can. Explain what is **urgent** (safety, such as brakes and steering), what can **wait**, and what it costs to delay."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Estimates and approval"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Give an estimate before starting. State that it is an estimate and what may change it.","If you find extra work, **stop and ask for approval** before you continue.","Be honest. Do not sell parts or work that are not needed, and do not charge for work that you did not do."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Managing expectations"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Give a realistic completion time. If there is a delay, such as a part that is late or a new fault, call the customer before they call you. Do not promise what you cannot deliver. When you hand back the vehicle, explain what was done, show the old parts if the customer wants, and tell them what to watch for."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Keep the customer's property safe too. Use seat and floor covers, remove personal items only with permission, and return the vehicle clean of oil and fingerprints. Small acts of care show that you respect the customer and their vehicle, and they are remembered long after the price is forgotten."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Handling a difficult conversation"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Situation","A good response"],"rows":[["The price is higher than the estimate","Call before you continue, explain what was found, give the new total and wait for approval"],["The customer is angry about a comeback","Listen without arguing, apologise for the inconvenience, book the vehicle in at once and find the cause"],["The customer says another workshop quoted less","Explain what is included in your price, such as parts quality and warranty. Do not criticise the other workshop."],["The customer wants a cheap repair on a safety part","Explain the risk clearly and in writing. Refuse to do a repair you know is unsafe."],["You made a mistake","Admit it, put it right at your cost and learn from it"]]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Honesty is the most valuable thing a workshop has. One dishonest job can lose the customer and everyone that they talk to."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"If you cannot fix a fault, say so and suggest a specialist. A customer respects honesty much more than a repair that fails."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m21-l6", "title": "Customer communication and professional conduct", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Technical skill gets a vehicle repaired. Communication gets the customer to come back. This lesson looks at the whole customer journey, from the first call to the follow-up, and at the standards of conduct that make a technician trusted."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The customer journey"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Stage","What the customer wants","What you do"],"rows":[["Booking","A clear answer about time and a rough price","Ask for the vehicle, the symptom and any warning lights"],["Arrival","To be greeted and to feel listened to","Walk round the vehicle, note existing damage and fuel level, and record the mileage"],["Diagnosis","To know what is wrong and what it costs","Explain the findings plainly, with a written estimate"],["Repair","Updates if there is a delay","Call before they call you. Get approval for extra work."],["Handover","A clean vehicle and an explanation","Show what was done, return old parts, explain the warranty and any advice"],["Follow-up","To know it is still all right","Call after a few days to ask if the vehicle is working as expected"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Writing an estimate"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["List the labour and parts separately, with part numbers.","Say how long the estimate is valid, and what could change it.","Separate the **urgent** safety work from the work that can wait.","Give the customer a copy, and keep one on the job card."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Explaining with an analogy"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Part","Plain-words comparison"],"rows":[["Engine oil","The blood of the engine. It carries away heat and keeps parts from touching."],["Brake fluid","The messenger from your foot to the brakes. If it has water in it, the message gets weak."],["Timing belt","The link that keeps the valves and pistons in step. If it breaks, the engine can be wrecked."],["Battery","The store of energy. As it gets old, it holds less."],["Wheel alignment","Making all four wheels point the same way, like straight lines on a road."]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Professional conduct"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Be on time** and keep promises. If you cannot, say so early.","**Be honest** about your own mistakes, and about what you do not know.","**Respect the vehicle:** use covers, keep the cabin clean, and do not use the vehicle for your own purposes.","**Protect private information:** a customer's address, phone number and records are private. Do not share them.","**Be fair:** do not overcharge, do not charge for work not done, and do not accept gifts that influence your advice.","**Work as a team:** share information, help others and accept correction."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Dealing with complaints"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Listen without interrupting. Let the person finish.","Say that you understand, and thank them for telling you.","Do not blame others. Ask what they would like done.","Look at the vehicle at once, and find the true cause.","Tell the customer what you found and what you will do, and when.","Follow up to be sure it is settled. Record the outcome."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Trust is built by listening, explaining, being honest and following up."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"A short written note saying what was done and what to watch for takes a minute and prevents many questions later."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m21-l7", "title": "Job cards, records and parts", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A job card is the record of the whole job. A good one protects the customer, the workshop and you."},
      {"t":"h","text":"What a job card contains"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Customer name, contact, vehicle make, model, registration, mileage and date.","The complaint, in the customer's words.","Findings: codes, readings and inspection results.","Work done, with times, and the technician who did it.","Parts used, with part numbers and prices, and any warranty.","Test results and the final road test.","Extra work recommended, and what the customer approved or refused."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Write clearly and at the time. A vague note such as 'fixed brakes' is not enough. 'Replaced front pads and discs, bled system, tested at low speed, pedal firm' is."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Parts sourcing"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Type","Advantages","Cautions"],"rows":[["New original","Correct fit and quality, often with a warranty","Highest price"],["New aftermarket","Lower price, good quality from a good maker","Quality varies. Choose a known brand"],["Used","Low price, can be the only source for old models","Unknown history. Inspect it, and avoid safety parts such as airbags and brake parts"],["Reconditioned","Cheaper than new, such as alternators and steering racks","Quality depends on the reconditioner. Ask for a warranty"],["Counterfeit","None","Fake parts fail quickly and can be dangerous. Buy from trusted suppliers and check packaging"]],"caption":"Parts types"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Warranty, stock and returns"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Keep receipts and part numbers so that a faulty part can be returned under warranty. Store stock in a clear, dry and organised way and keep a record of what you have. Return wrong or faulty parts quickly and label them. Never refit a part that was returned as faulty."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Keep the records for a reasonable time, because customers return with questions about earlier work. Digital records on a phone or a computer are easy to search, but keep a backup."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"A clear job card is your proof. If a customer comes back with a dispute, the card shows what was found, what was done and what was approved."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Do not use fake or unknown safety parts such as brake pads, tyres, airbags or steering parts. A cheap counterfeit part can cost a life."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m21-l8", "title": "Costing and running the workshop", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A workshop that cannot cover its costs will close, however good the mechanics are. Costing is not greed. It is what keeps the doors open and the staff paid."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Labour"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Labour is charged by the hour at the workshop rate. Many workshops use **book times**, which are standard times for a job published by the maker or a guide, and not the clock time of the mechanic. Diagnosis is also a service and takes time, so it should have a price."},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Estimate = (labour hours × hourly rate) + parts + consumables + tax","caption":"Consumables are small items such as oil, cleaner and rags. Taxes, such as VAT, depend on the rules in force."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Worked example: brake pad replacement","steps":["Book time: 1.5 hours at GHS 100 per hour, labour GHS 150.","Parts: pads GHS 220. Brake cleaner and grease GHS 20.","Subtotal: 150 + 220 + 20 = GHS 390. Add any tax that applies.","The final figure is written on the estimate and approved by the customer before work begins."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Invoices and payments"},
      {"t":"p","text":"An invoice lists the work, the parts and the total. Offer a receipt. Many customers pay by **mobile money** or by cash or by bank. Record each payment against the job and keep the records for tax purposes. Never keep two sets of figures."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Profit and comebacks"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Profit is what remains after paying parts, wages, rent, electricity, tools and other costs. A **comeback** is a vehicle that returns with the same fault after repair. Comebacks are costly because the work is repeated for nothing and the customer loses trust. They come from rushed diagnosis, bad parts, missed final checks and poor communication. Reduce them by following the six steps, using good parts, doing the final road test and telling the customer what to expect."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Price your time and your knowledge as well as your parts. A workshop that charges too little cannot buy tools or train staff."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Keep a list of common jobs with book times and your own times. After some months, you will see the real cost of each job."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m21-l9", "title": "Costing, efficiency and quality control", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A workshop is a small business. The technician who understands costs, time and quality helps the workshop to grow, and is more valuable to it. This lesson takes the costing of a job further, and looks at efficiency and quality checks."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Where the hourly rate comes from"},
      {"t":"example","title":"A simple workshop rate","steps":["Monthly costs: rent 3,000, electricity and water 800, wages for 3 technicians 9,000, tools and equipment 1,200, insurance and other 1,000. The total is 15,000.","Each technician works 8 hours a day, 24 days a month, which is 192 hours. But only about 75% of that time is billed, so 144 billable hours each. For three technicians: 432 hours.","Cost per billable hour = 15,000 ÷ 432 = 34.7. To make a profit of about 30% on the labour, charge 34.7 × 1.3 = 45 per hour.","This shows why rates must cover rent, wages and equipment, as well as the technician's own time, and why unbilled time costs money."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Efficiency and productivity"},
      {"t":"formula","text":"Efficiency (%) = book hours billed ÷ hours worked × 100","caption":"A technician who bills 7 book hours in an 8-hour day has an efficiency of 87.5%"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Cause of lost time","How to reduce it"],"rows":[["Searching for tools or parts","Keep tools organised, and parts clearly labelled"],["Waiting for approval","Ask the customer for contact details and approval early"],["Repeated work (comebacks)","Follow the six steps and check the work"],["Poor planning","Plan the day. Order parts when you diagnose, not after."],["Lack of information","Use the repair database. Keep manuals handy."]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Margin on parts"},
      {"t":"example","title":"Part mark-up","steps":["A part costs 220. The workshop adds a 25% margin: 220 × 1.25 = 275.","This covers the cost of ordering, stocking, returns and warranty handling. It is normal, if it is openly stated.","If the customer supplies the part, the workshop may charge for labour but give no warranty on the part. Explain this first."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Quality control"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["**Check the work before the customer does.** After every job, look at your work again with fresh eyes.","**Use a checklist:** torque on wheel nuts, fluid levels, no leaks, clean vehicle, warning lights off.","**Road test** every vehicle after repairs to steering, brakes, suspension or the driveline.","**Get a second check** on safety-critical work: brakes, steering, wheels and restraints.","**Record comebacks** and find the cause. Look for patterns.","**Ask for feedback** from customers and use it."]},
      {"t":"example","title":"The cost of a comeback","steps":["A repair earned 400. The vehicle comes back after a week for the same fault. The re-check and the repair take 3 hours, and the part costs 150. The shop gives them free.","The cost is 3 × 34.7 + 150 = 254. This wipes out most of the profit on the first job.","Add the cost of lost trust, and a comeback is among the most expensive things a workshop can have."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Know your costs, plan your time and check your work. Quality is cheaper than repeating the job."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m21-l10", "title": "Professional development", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Vehicles change quickly. A mechanic who stops learning will soon be left behind. The best mechanics treat their career as a long course."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Staying current"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Read the manuals and bulletins for each new vehicle model you work on.","Learn about hybrid and electric vehicles, driver assistance systems and new networks. Many of these need training and special safety rules.","Take short courses, attend maker training and join trade groups.","Learn from experienced colleagues and ask questions."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Safety culture"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Safety is a habit. Wear the right protective clothing, keep the workshop tidy, use lifts and stands correctly, know where the fire extinguisher and first aid kit are, and dispose of oil, coolant, refrigerant and batteries correctly. Report near misses so others can learn. Never take a short cut that you would not want a trainee to copy."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Mentoring juniors"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Teach by showing, explaining why and then watching the learner do the job. Be patient with errors, since they are how people learn. Make sure they never work alone on safety-critical jobs until they are competent. Give them small, clear tasks first, check each result, and praise what is done well before you correct what is wrong. Ask them to explain a job back to you in their own words, since that shows whether they understood or only copied. Share your own mistakes openly, because a junior who sees that experts also make errors will report problems early instead of hiding them. Over time, give more responsibility step by step, and record what each person has shown that they can do safely."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Certification and routes"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Training and certification routes differ by country. In Ghana, the national technical and vocational bodies, such as COTVET and the NVTI proficiency tests, assess skills, and many makers and training centres give their own certificates. Ask your trainer or the local authority which route fits you, and keep your certificates and a record of the jobs you have done."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Tools, ethics and law"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Look after your tools: clean them, store them in order and calibrate torque wrenches and gauges. Work ethically: be honest with customers, do not take bribes, do not tamper with odometers or emission systems, and respect the law about waste, refrigerants and safety. Your name is your reputation."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"The best mechanics are the ones who never stop learning and never stop checking their own work."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Each month, learn one new thing about a system you do not know well, and write it in your notebook."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m21-l11", "title": "Workshop regulations, environment and ethics", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A workshop deals with hazardous materials, safety-critical repairs and valuable property. Laws and good practice protect workers, customers and the environment. This lesson gives the principles. Details vary by country, so check the rules where you work."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Waste handling"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Waste","Hazard","Correct handling"],"rows":[["Used engine oil","Poisons water. Skin contact can be harmful.","Collect in a labelled sealed container, store on a drip tray, and send to an approved collector"],["Coolant","Poisonous, and sweet to animals and children","Collect, label, and send for recycling or disposal"],["Brake fluid","Harms paintwork and is toxic","Collect separately in a sealed container"],["Lead-acid batteries","Acid and lead","Store upright under cover, and return to the supplier or a recycler"],["Refrigerant","Harms the atmosphere","Recover with certified equipment by a trained person. Never vent."],["Used filters and oily rags","Fire hazard and oil","Drain the filters, store in a metal bin with a lid, and dispose of safely"],["Tyres","Fire hazard and a breeding place for insects","Store under cover and send to a recycler"],["Brake dust","Harmful to breathe","Use wet cleaning or a vacuum, never compressed air"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Workplace safety duties"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Know where the **fire extinguishers, first aid kit, eyewash and exits** are, and how to use them.","Wear **personal protective equipment** suitable for the job: eye protection, gloves, boots, hearing protection and overalls.","Keep floors **clean and dry**. Clean spills at once with absorbent.","Use **lifting equipment** with the right rating, and check it before use.","Keep **chemical safety data sheets** where they can be found.","Report accidents, near misses and unsafe conditions.","Do not work when tired, unwell or under the influence of alcohol or drugs."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Legal and ethical responsibilities"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Issue","What is right"],"rows":[["Odometer","Never alter a mileage reading. It is fraud."],["Emission systems","Never remove or disable a catalyst, particulate filter or other emission part"],["Safety defects","Tell the customer in writing, and do not hide them"],["Warranty work","Do only the work that is covered and record it truthfully"],["Stolen or suspicious vehicles and parts","Do not accept them. Report suspicious cases as the law requires."],["Customer records","Keep private, and do not share without permission"],["Unlicensed work","Do not work on areas, such as high-voltage or refrigerant, that you are not qualified to handle"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Making a career plan"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["List the skills you have now, and the skills the jobs you want need.","Choose the next skill to learn, and a date by which you will have it.","Find a course, a mentor or a manual that can teach it.","Keep a record of jobs, certificates and references.","Review the plan every six months."]},
      {"t":"example","title":"A sample three-year path","steps":["**Year 1:** Complete basic training. Be able to service, brake, suspension and tyre work safely.","**Year 2:** Learn engine management and electrical diagnosis. Take a recognised test.","**Year 3:** Train in hybrid and electric vehicle safety, or advanced driver assistance, as the market in your area demands. Begin to mentor juniors."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Obey the law, protect the environment and your team, and always be honest. These habits are what make a professional."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"If you are not trained for a task such as high-voltage work, refrigerant recovery or airbag disposal, do not attempt it. Ask a trained person or send the vehicle to a specialist."}
    ]}
  ],
  "practical": {"title":"Diagnose a seeded fault and write a job card","objective":"Diagnose a seeded fault on a training vehicle or rig using the six steps, and write a complete job card.","time":"120 minutes","tools":["Training vehicle or rig with a seeded fault set by the instructor","Scan tool, multimeter and basic hand tools","Workshop manual or repair database and wiring diagram","A blank job card and a notebook"],"safety":["Chock the wheels and apply the handbrake before the engine is started.","Do not work on airbag or high-voltage systems. Ask the instructor.","Keep clear of belts and fans while the engine runs.","Wear eye protection and gloves. Tie back long hair and remove jewellery."],"steps":["Read the customer's complaint on the job card and repeat it in your own words.","Verify the complaint by seeing or hearing the fault.","Gather information: the history, the codes, any bulletins and the conditions of the fault.","Check the obvious items: battery, fluids, fuses, connectors and leaks. Note the results.","Plan a test using the manual and divide the system into two halves.","Measure the values and compare them with the specification. Record each one.","Identify the cause and explain why you are sure.","Repair or replace the faulty part as the instructor allows, using the correct procedure.","Confirm the repair with a test and clear any codes only after the repair.","Write the complete job card with the complaint, findings, work, parts and test.","Explain the fault and the repair to the instructor as if to a customer."],"checks":["I followed the six steps in order.","I recorded the measurements and compared them with the specification.","I did not replace a part without a test that showed it was faulty.","I confirmed the repair.","My job card is clear and complete, and my explanation was in plain words."]},
  "quiz": [
    {"id":"m21-q01","q":"What is the first step of the six-step diagnostic method?","options":["Replace the part that is most likely to be faulty on the vehicle","Verify the complaint by seeing or hearing the fault","Write the estimate and ask the customer to approve it","Clear all of the fault codes and see which ones return"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m21-l1","explain":"A fault that cannot be reproduced is hard to fix, so confirm it first."},
    {"id":"m21-q02","q":"What is the difference between a symptom and a cause?","options":["A symptom is the cost of the repair and a cause is the labour time","They are the same thing described in two different ways by the trade","A symptom is what is noticed, and a cause is the fault in the part or circuit","A symptom is a fault code and a cause is a warning light on the dash"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l1","explain":"Many different causes can give the same symptom, so test before replacing."},
    {"id":"m21-q03","q":"What does 'check the obvious' include?","options":["Replacing the engine control unit with a new one","Fluids, fuses, connectors, battery condition and leaks","Removing the engine and checking the bearings first","Asking the customer to take the car to another workshop"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m21-l1","explain":"Many faults have simple causes, which are quick and cheap to check."},
    {"id":"m21-q04","q":"A fault code points to a sensor. What is the best action?","options":["Clear the code and let the customer drive the car away","Test the sensor, its wiring and connector, and the real fault it sees","Replace the engine control unit because it stores the code","Replace the sensor at once, because the code names that sensor"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m21-l1","explain":"A code is a clue and not a verdict. The wire, the connector or the real fault may be the cause."},
    {"id":"m21-q05","q":"Why is parts-swapping a poor method?","options":["It makes the vehicle lose its warranty with the maker at once","It is always forbidden by law in every country of the world","It is expensive, it can hide the real fault and it teaches nothing","It is slow only because the new parts are hard to find in the shop"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l1","explain":"Before you replace a part, you should have a measurement that shows it is faulty."},
    {"id":"m21-q06","q":"What is the last step in the six-step method?","options":["Write the invoice and send the customer home at once","Clear the codes and put the vehicle outside in the yard","Confirm that the fault is gone and no new fault appears","Replace all of the parts near the original fault to be safe"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l1","explain":"Repeat the test or the road test and check that the repair is lasting."},
    {"id":"m21-q07","q":"Ten possible faults are tested by halving the search each time. About how many tests are needed to find one?","options":["About ten","About one","About forty","About four"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m21-l2","explain":"Each test halves the list: 10, 5, 3, 2, 1. So about four tests."},
    {"id":"m21-q08","q":"A cranking battery falls to 8.1 V. What does this suggest?","options":["A perfectly good battery","A bad fuel pump relay","A weak battery or poor connection","A blown headlamp bulb"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l2","explain":"The voltage should stay above about 9.6 V while cranking. A low reading needs a voltage drop test and a battery test."},
    {"id":"m21-q09","q":"A long-term fuel trim of +22% suggests what?","options":["Extra fuel is being added","The engine is running rich","The oil is overfilled","The battery is flat"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l2","explain":"A large positive trim means the mixture is lean, often from an air leak, so the computer adds fuel."},
    {"id":"m21-q10","q":"Why use a smoke machine on the intake?","options":["To clean the engine","To find air leaks","To warm the cylinders","To test the starter"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l2","explain":"Smoke escapes from a leak so that a small crack in a hose or gasket becomes visible."},
    {"id":"m21-q11","q":"What is a common cause of an intermittent no-start with no codes?","options":["A bad radio fitted","A worn wiper blade","A loose connector","A low washer fluid level"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l2","explain":"Intermittent faults are often connections. Wiggle tests and data logging can help."},
    {"id":"m21-q12","q":"What should the last step of any repair be?","options":["Clear the history only","Confirm the fault is gone","Charge the customer first","Close the bonnet and leave"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m21-l2","explain":"A confirming test shows that the repair worked and that no new fault appeared."},
    {"id":"m21-q13","q":"Which source gives technical notes about known faults and their fixes?","options":["Technical service bulletins (TSBs)","The tyre placard in the door frame","The customer's invoice from the last service","The vehicle registration document"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m21-l3","explain":"Check for TSBs and recall notices in the gather-information step."},
    {"id":"m21-q14","q":"What does an oscilloscope show that a multimeter cannot?","options":["The battery voltage when the engine is off","The resistance of a wire in ohms","The fuel level in the tank","A signal as a graph over time"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l3","explain":"It is used for fast signals such as sensors, injectors and a CAN bus."},
    {"id":"m21-q15","q":"What should you check before blaming a computer for a strange fault?","options":["The colour of the wires and the age of the car on the register","The battery voltage, the earths and the connections","The number of kilometres since the last wash of the car","The brand of the engine oil in the sump of the engine"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m21-l3","explain":"Low voltage and bad earths cause a lot of odd faults on modern cars."},
    {"id":"m21-q16","q":"A fault happens only once or twice a day. What helps most?","options":["Data logging to capture the fault, then studying the record","Clearing the codes and hoping that the fault does not return","Replacing parts one by one until the fault stops happening","Telling the customer that the fault does not really exist"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l3","explain":"A recording lets you see what changed when the fault happened."},
    {"id":"m21-q17","q":"What is a recall notice?","options":["A letter from the customer about the quality of a repair","A list of workshop prices published by the maker each year","A reminder that the next service is due on the vehicle soon","A safety defect that the maker repairs free of charge"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m21-l3","explain":"Always check for recalls on a vehicle, because the repair is free to the customer."},
    {"id":"m21-q18","q":"When should you escalate a fault to a senior or a specialist?","options":["When it is beyond your training, such as high voltage or airbags","When the vehicle is dirty and needs a wash before work","When you do not like the make or the colour of the vehicle","When the customer asks a question in the workshop office"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l3","explain":"Knowing your limits is part of professional practice."},
    {"id":"m21-q19","q":"Where is a voltage drop test meter placed?","options":["Across the part, under load","Across the battery only","Across the load, power off","From the wire to earth only"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l4","explain":"The drop is measured across the wire, switch or connection under load to find where the loss is."},
    {"id":"m21-q20","q":"A headlamp draws 4.6 A and the earth wire drops 1.15 V. What is its resistance?","options":["About 5.3 ohm","About 0.025 ohm","About 25 ohm","About 0.25 ohm"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m21-l4","explain":"Resistance = voltage ÷ current = 1.15 ÷ 4.6 = 0.25 Ω, far too high for a wire."},
    {"id":"m21-q21","q":"What is the typical acceptable drop on the earth cable during cranking?","options":["Under about 5 V","Under about 0.2 V","Exactly zero volts","Above 3 V in total"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l4","explain":"A larger drop shows a poor earth connection or a worn cable."},
    {"id":"m21-q22","q":"What can an oscilloscope show that a meter cannot?","options":["The brake fluid level","The tyre pressure in kPa","The colour of the oil","The shape of a fast signal"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m21-l4","explain":"A scope draws voltage against time, so it shows drop-outs, noise and waveform shape."},
    {"id":"m21-q23","q":"A scan tool shows a coolant temperature of −40 °C on a warm engine. What is likely?","options":["An open circuit or bad sensor","A very cold engine block","A perfectly good sensor","A full fuel tank"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l4","explain":"A reading at the extreme end of the range often means an open or short circuit, not a real temperature."},
    {"id":"m21-q24","q":"Why use freeze frame data?","options":["It resets all the codes","It tests the battery health","Shows conditions at the fault","It prints the invoice"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l4","explain":"Speed, load and temperature at the time of the fault help to reproduce it."},
    {"id":"m21-q25","q":"What is the best way to start the conversation with a customer?","options":["Tell them what you think is wrong before they begin to speak","Look at the car and only talk to them when you are finished","Ask for the money for the diagnosis before anything else","Greet them and let them tell the story without interrupting"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m21-l5","explain":"Listening gives you the clues you need and builds trust."},
    {"id":"m21-q26","q":"A customer asks why the brake pads need to be replaced. How should you explain?","options":["By saying that all cars need it after one year of use","By telling them that it is a secret of the trade","With technical terms, so that they trust your knowledge of cars","In plain words with an everyday comparison, and show the worn part"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m21-l5","explain":"Plain words and a sight of the old part build trust."},
    {"id":"m21-q27","q":"You find extra work needed during a repair. What do you do?","options":["Ignore it and say nothing in order to finish on time","Do the work and tell the customer when they collect the car","Stop and ask for the customer's approval before continuing","Do the work and add it to the bill without telling them"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l5","explain":"Never add work or cost without approval."},
    {"id":"m21-q28","q":"A part you ordered will arrive two days late. What should you do?","options":["Fit a used part of any kind and hope it will work","Tell the customer early and give a new realistic time","Tell the customer that the job is complete and wait","Say nothing and hope that it arrives sooner than expected"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l5","explain":"Call before the customer calls you, and give a new realistic time for collection."},
    {"id":"m21-q29","q":"You cannot fix a fault. What is the right thing to do?","options":["Charge for the hours and say it is fixed","Say so honestly and suggest a specialist","Blame the previous workshop without any proof","Replace parts at random until the customer leaves"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m21-l5","explain":"Honesty is respected more than a repair that fails."},
    {"id":"m21-q30","q":"The price is higher than the estimate. What should you do first?","options":["Continue and explain later","Ignore the extra cost","Hide the invoice from them","Get approval first"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l6","explain":"The customer must approve extra work in advance. Explain what was found, and the new total."},
    {"id":"m21-q31","q":"What is a good way to respond to a customer complaint?","options":["Interrupt to defend yourself","Walk away","Listen without interrupting","Blame a colleague"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m21-l6","explain":"Listening shows respect and helps you find the true problem."},
    {"id":"m21-q32","q":"What does a good estimate include?","options":["Labour and parts listed","Only a total with no detail","A rough guess by phone","Nothing written down"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l6","explain":"Separate figures let the customer see what is paid for, and help avoid disputes."},
    {"id":"m21-q33","q":"A customer asks for a cheap repair on a worn brake part. What should you do?","options":["Do it quietly and say nothing","Refuse an unsafe repair","Do only half of the job","Charge double and do it"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l6","explain":"You must not do or hide an unsafe repair. Explain clearly and record it in writing."},
    {"id":"m21-q34","q":"What is the purpose of a job card?","options":["To show the price of every part in the store room","To advertise the workshop to new customers in the area","To record the complaint, findings, work, parts and tests","To list the staff and their working hours for the week"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l7","explain":"It protects the customer, the workshop and you."},
    {"id":"m21-q35","q":"Which job card note is the best?","options":["Replaced front pads and discs, bled system, tested at low speed, pedal firm","Brakes done and OK","Fixed brakes","Did the car, no problems"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m21-l7","explain":"A good note says what was found, what was done and what was checked."},
    {"id":"m21-q36","q":"Which type of part is the most dangerous to buy from an unknown seller?","options":["A new original oil filter in a sealed box","A reconditioned alternator with a warranty","A new aftermarket air filter from a known brand","A counterfeit brake pad or airbag"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l7","explain":"Fake safety parts fail and can cost lives."},
    {"id":"m21-q37","q":"Why keep receipts and part numbers?","options":["So a faulty part can be returned under warranty and the repair can be traced","So the workshop can charge the customer twice for the same part","So the customer can keep the receipts for many years","So the parts can be refitted to the next vehicle that comes in"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m21-l7","explain":"They also help to answer later disputes."},
    {"id":"m21-q38","q":"A returned part was marked faulty by the supplier. What must you never do?","options":["Return it to the supplier within the time limit","Label it and store it in a separate place","Record it on the job card","Refit it to a vehicle"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l7","explain":"Faulty parts must be removed from the stock."},
    {"id":"m21-q39","q":"What is book time?","options":["The time that the customer waits in the office for the car","The time that the mechanic took on the day of the repair","The standard time for a job from a guide or the maker","The time between two services of the same vehicle"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m21-l8","explain":"Many workshops charge book time instead of the clock time."},
    {"id":"m21-q40","q":"Book time is 2 hours at GHS 80 per hour, and parts cost GHS 150. What is the subtotal before tax?","options":["GHS 310","GHS 230","GHS 390","GHS 160"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m21-l8","explain":"Labour is 2 × 80 = 160, and 160 + 150 = 310."},
    {"id":"m21-q41","q":"What is a comeback?","options":["A vehicle that is brought in for its regular service","A vehicle that returns with the same fault after a repair","A part that is returned to the supplier under warranty","A customer who comes back to the forecourt to buy more fuel"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l8","explain":"Comebacks cost the workshop money and cost the customer's trust in the repair."},
    {"id":"m21-q42","q":"Which action reduces comebacks?","options":["Working faster so that the job is finished sooner than booked","Telling the customer to come back later if it fails again","Using the cheapest parts to keep the bill as low as possible","Following the six steps and doing a final road test"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l8","explain":"Most comebacks come from rushed diagnosis or missed checks."},
    {"id":"m21-q43","q":"A customer pays by mobile money. What should you do?","options":["Record the payment against the job and give a receipt","Ask the customer to pay again in cash to be sure","Keep the payment off the books in order to save some tax","Wait a month before recording it against the job"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m21-l8","explain":"Records must be accurate and complete, so that the books and the job agree."},
    {"id":"m21-q44","q":"Why should a workshop charge for diagnosis?","options":["Because customers like paying for everything that is on the bill","Because the scan tool is free to use for every workshop","It takes skill, time and equipment, which must be paid for","Because the manual is not needed for good diagnosis"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l8","explain":"Diagnosis is a service and has real costs."},
    {"id":"m21-q45","q":"Monthly costs are 15,000 and the shop has 432 billable hours. What is the cost per hour?","options":["About 3.5 per hour","About 347 per hour","About 34.7","About 150 per hour"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m21-l9","explain":"Cost per billable hour is the monthly costs divided by the billable hours: 15,000 divided by 432 gives about 34.7."},
    {"id":"m21-q46","q":"A technician bills 7 book hours in an 8-hour day. What is the efficiency?","options":["70%","87.5%","115%","8.75%"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l9","explain":"Efficiency is book hours billed divided by hours worked, times 100. Here 7 divided by 8 gives 87.5 percent."},
    {"id":"m21-q47","q":"A part costs 220 and the margin is 25%. What is the price?","options":["275","245","220","440"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l9","explain":"Add the margin to the cost: 220 times 1.25 gives 275. The margin covers ordering, stocking and warranty handling."},
    {"id":"m21-q48","q":"Why is a comeback costly?","options":["It is free for the customer","It saves the shop time","It adds extra profit","The work is repeated unpaid"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l9","explain":"The shop pays for labour and parts again and loses trust."},
    {"id":"m21-q49","q":"Which is the best way to stay current with new vehicle technology?","options":["Rely only on what you learned during your first year of training","Take courses, read the manuals and bulletins and learn from colleagues","Avoid new models and work only on old cars for ever","Copy what other workshops charge without learning anything"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m21-l10","explain":"Vehicles change quickly and the learning never stops."},
    {"id":"m21-q50","q":"How should you teach a junior mechanic a new task?","options":["Tell them to guess and learn from the customer's reaction","Let them work alone on the brakes on their first day","Show it, explain why, and then watch them do it","Do the job quickly so they can see how fast it is"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l10","explain":"Never leave juniors alone on safety-critical jobs until they are competent."},
    {"id":"m21-q51","q":"Which action is unethical and illegal?","options":["Tampering with an odometer or an emission system","Disposing of old oil at an approved collection place","Giving a written estimate before starting the work on the vehicle","Refusing to fit a counterfeit part that a customer brought"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m21-l10","explain":"Honesty and respect for the law protect your reputation."},
    {"id":"m21-q52","q":"How should used engine oil be handled?","options":["Send to an approved collector","Pour it on the ground","Pour it down a drain","Burn it in the open air"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m21-l11","explain":"Oil poisons soil and water. Store it in a sealed, labelled container for collection."},
    {"id":"m21-q53","q":"What must be done with refrigerant?","options":["Vent it into the air","Pour it down a drain","Mix it with engine oil","Recover it with certified kit"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m21-l11","explain":"Refrigerant harms the atmosphere, so only trained people recover it with proper equipment."},
    {"id":"m21-q54","q":"Is it acceptable to change an odometer reading?","options":["Yes, for a small fee","No, it is fraud","Yes, if the owner asks","Yes, for old cars"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m21-l11","explain":"Mileage tampering is illegal and dishonest, and it misleads future buyers."},
    {"id":"m21-q55","q":"What should you do if you are not trained for a task such as high-voltage work?","options":["Try it very carefully","Ask a friend to help","Refer it on","Skip the safety checks"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m21-l11","explain":"Untrained work on high voltage or airbags can kill. Always escalate to a trained person."}
  ],
  "cards": [
    {"id":"m21-c01","front":"What are the six diagnostic steps?","back":"Verify the complaint, gather information, check the obvious, test and isolate, repair, confirm.","lesson":"m21-l1"},
    {"id":"m21-c02","front":"What is a symptom?","back":"What the customer notices, such as hard starting.","lesson":"m21-l1"},
    {"id":"m21-c03","front":"What is a cause?","back":"The fault in the part or circuit that creates the symptom.","lesson":"m21-l1"},
    {"id":"m21-c04","front":"Is a fault code a verdict?","back":"No. It is a clue to be tested.","lesson":"m21-l1"},
    {"id":"m21-c05","front":"Why avoid parts-swapping?","back":"It is costly, can hide the real fault and teaches nothing.","lesson":"m21-l1"},
    {"id":"m21-c06","front":"How many tests to narrow 10 faults?","back":"About four, by halving the possibilities each time.","lesson":"m21-l2"},
    {"id":"m21-c07","front":"Cranking voltage limit?","back":"It should stay above about 9.6 V while cranking.","lesson":"m21-l2"},
    {"id":"m21-c08","front":"What does +22% long-term trim mean?","back":"Lean mixture. The computer is adding fuel, often for an air leak.","lesson":"m21-l2"},
    {"id":"m21-c09","front":"What finds an air leak?","back":"A smoke machine on the intake.","lesson":"m21-l2"},
    {"id":"m21-c10","front":"What is a TSB?","back":"A technical service bulletin about a known fault and its fix.","lesson":"m21-l3"},
    {"id":"m21-c11","front":"What does a scope show?","back":"A signal as a graph over time.","lesson":"m21-l3"},
    {"id":"m21-c12","front":"What should you check before blaming an ECU?","back":"Battery voltage, earths and connections.","lesson":"m21-l3"},
    {"id":"m21-c13","front":"What is data logging for?","back":"To capture a fault that comes and goes.","lesson":"m21-l3"},
    {"id":"m21-c14","front":"When do you escalate?","back":"When the fault is beyond your training, such as HV or airbags.","lesson":"m21-l3"},
    {"id":"m21-c15","front":"What is a voltage drop test?","back":"Measuring the voltage lost across a part while current flows through it.","lesson":"m21-l4"},
    {"id":"m21-c16","front":"Earth cable drop limit?","back":"Under about 0.2 V during cranking.","lesson":"m21-l4"},
    {"id":"m21-c17","front":"What is freeze frame data?","back":"The conditions stored when a fault was logged.","lesson":"m21-l4"},
    {"id":"m21-c18","front":"Why use a scope?","back":"It shows the shape of a signal over time, including drop-outs and noise.","lesson":"m21-l4"},
    {"id":"m21-c19","front":"How do you start with a customer?","back":"Greet them and listen without interrupting.","lesson":"m21-l5"},
    {"id":"m21-c20","front":"What do you do with extra work found?","back":"Stop and ask for approval.","lesson":"m21-l5"},
    {"id":"m21-c21","front":"How do you explain a fault?","back":"In plain words, and show the old part.","lesson":"m21-l5"},
    {"id":"m21-c22","front":"What if you cannot fix it?","back":"Say so and suggest a specialist.","lesson":"m21-l5"},
    {"id":"m21-c23","front":"What do you do about extra work?","back":"Stop, explain, give the new price and wait for approval.","lesson":"m21-l6"},
    {"id":"m21-c24","front":"How to handle an angry customer?","back":"Listen, apologise for the inconvenience, find the true cause and follow up.","lesson":"m21-l6"},
    {"id":"m21-c25","front":"What goes on a job card?","back":"Complaint, findings, work, parts, tests and approvals.","lesson":"m21-l7"},
    {"id":"m21-c26","front":"What is the risk of a counterfeit part?","back":"It can fail quickly and is dangerous.","lesson":"m21-l7"},
    {"id":"m21-c27","front":"Why keep part numbers and receipts?","back":"For warranty returns and traceability.","lesson":"m21-l7"},
    {"id":"m21-c28","front":"Which used parts should be avoided?","back":"Safety parts such as airbags and brake parts.","lesson":"m21-l7"},
    {"id":"m21-c29","front":"What is book time?","back":"The standard time for a job.","lesson":"m21-l8"},
    {"id":"m21-c30","front":"What is the estimate formula?","back":"Labour hours × rate + parts + consumables + tax.","lesson":"m21-l8"},
    {"id":"m21-c31","front":"What is a comeback?","back":"A vehicle that returns with the same fault.","lesson":"m21-l8"},
    {"id":"m21-c32","front":"How do you reduce comebacks?","back":"Follow the six steps, use good parts and do a final test.","lesson":"m21-l8"},
    {"id":"m21-c33","front":"How do you record mobile money?","back":"Against the job, with a receipt.","lesson":"m21-l8"},
    {"id":"m21-c34","front":"Efficiency formula?","back":"Book hours billed divided by hours worked, times 100.","lesson":"m21-l9"},
    {"id":"m21-c35","front":"Cost per hour?","back":"Total monthly costs divided by billable hours.","lesson":"m21-l9"},
    {"id":"m21-c36","front":"Part margin example?","back":"A cost of 220 with a 25% margin sells at 275.","lesson":"m21-l9"},
    {"id":"m21-c37","front":"How do you teach a junior?","back":"Show it, explain why, watch them do it.","lesson":"m21-l10"},
    {"id":"m21-c38","front":"What is an ethical rule on odometers?","back":"Never tamper with them.","lesson":"m21-l10"},
    {"id":"m21-c39","front":"How do you stay current?","back":"Courses, manuals, bulletins and colleagues.","lesson":"m21-l10"},
    {"id":"m21-c40","front":"Why report near misses?","back":"So others can learn and the workshop stays safe.","lesson":"m21-l10"},
    {"id":"m21-c41","front":"What is the best habit of a good mechanic?","back":"Never stop learning and checking their own work.","lesson":"m21-l10"},
    {"id":"m21-c42","front":"Where does used oil go?","back":"Into a labelled sealed container for an approved collector.","lesson":"m21-l11"},
    {"id":"m21-c43","front":"Who recovers refrigerant?","back":"A trained person with certified equipment.","lesson":"m21-l11"},
    {"id":"m21-c44","front":"Can you change an odometer?","back":"No. It is fraud.","lesson":"m21-l11"}
  ]
});
